a skipped byte is still in the pixel, and no byte order means the natural one

Four defects in the 32 bit bitmap path, found by disassembling a shipping application rather than by
reading this code, because from inside every one of them looked like the application misbehaving.

ONE. CGBitmapContextGetBitsPerPixel answered 24 for kCGImageAlphaNoneSkipFirst and NoneSkipLast. The
skipped byte is still IN the pixel: an 8 bit per component RGB context with a skipped alpha is 32
bits wide, which is what its own bytesPerRow says and what macOS reports. AlphaOnly answered the
colour space width instead of one component.

TWO. kCGBitmapByteOrderDefault, which is 0 and is what a caller who does not care passes, matched no
case in the reader table, so the surface refused to initialise. That refusal is INVISIBLE from
outside: O2BitmapContextCreate still answers a context, with a NULL surface behind it, and
CGBitmapContextGetData then hands back NULL. Default now means the order the alpha info already
names, which is what the Big cases spell.

THREE. kCGImageAlphaPremultipliedFirst had a reader for LITTLE byte order only, so ARGB, one of the
commonest 32 bit formats an application asks for, could not be read at all. kCGImageAlphaFirst had no
reader for either order. Three readers added, two of them premultiplying on the way in for the
straight formats.

FOUR. O2ImageRead_XRGB8888_to_argb8u took the PAD byte as alpha, so an image whose padding happened
to be zero came out fully transparent. Its little endian sibling BGRX8888 always had this right.

WHAT IT COST, and how it was found. Money Manager EX, a wxWidgets application, died with a SIGSEGV
in its own SVG rasteriser writing through a null pointer. wxBitmapRefData::GetDepth IS
CGBitmapContextGetBitsPerPixel, disassembled from the shipping binary:

  1006687f7: movq 0x48(%rbx), %rdi
  100668800: callq _CGBitmapContextGetBitsPerPixel

and wxBitmap::GetRawData returns NULL when that differs from the 32 it asked for. wx then
premultiplied an SVG straight into NULL plus three, which is why the fault address was 1.

AND THE REGISTERS HAD TO BE READ TWICE. The faulting store is movb %r9b, -0x2(%rdi,%r8,4) with RDI
equal to 3, and there is an addq $0x3, %rdi four instructions earlier that I missed the first time
and briefly concluded the pointer WAS three rather than null. The dump is the arbiter, and so is
reading all of it.

MEASURED: mmex now gets through wxBitmapBundle, creates its toolbar bitmaps at 737x38 and 744x38, and
has zero surface failures and zero null surfaces where it had one of each. It still does not open a
window and this does not claim it does.

NO REGRESSION, and this touches every application because every application decodes images. All five
roster applications driven and every capture LOOKED at, all identical to the run before this change.
The darling testsuite is unchanged at 64 passed, 1 failed, the failure being PubSub and unrelated.

